course: dictionary
lesson: 13955
title: Ligature: a tie, a joined letter, a clamp
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A ligature is a thread, string or band that ties something tightly. Doctors use one to close a blood vessel so it stops bleeding. «The nurse tied a ligature around the cut to stop the bleeding.» Here the tie is a string, not a word or a letter.
explain: In writing, a ligature is two letters joined into one shape. «In the old book, the letters f and i were joined together in the word «fine».» That joined shape is a ligature. You can see one in æ, as in «encyclopædia».
explain: Music uses the word too. On a clarinet, the ligature is the metal clamp that holds the reed in place. The word has three meanings, so the context tells you which one: blood and doctors, letters and books, or a clarinet and music.
fact: A ligature is a thread, string or band used to tie something tightly.
fact: In writing, a ligature is two letters joined into one shape, such as æ or œ.
fact: «fi» in printed books is often a ligature, so the f and the i are joined together.
fact: On a clarinet, the ligature is the clamp that holds the reed.
fact: This is a formal word, so the topic around it is your best clue to its meaning.
exercise: «The surgeon tied a ligature around the vein.» What is a ligature here? | A metal letter | A kind of blood | *A thread or band tied tightly | A loud musical note
exercise: What do we call two letters joined into one shape, like æ? | A tight knot of blood | A clarinet reed | *A ligature | A type of music
exercise: Ellie's clarinet reed slipped, so her teacher tightened the metal clamp. What is that clamp called? | *A ligature | A drumbeat | A tie | A capital letter
test: «The doctor used a ligature to stop the bleeding.» What does ligature mean here? | A pencil | A kind of medicine | A type of music | *A string or band tied tightly
test: In some old printed books, «fi» is written as one joined shape. What is this called? | A blood vessel | A mark on a page | *A ligature | A ligament
exam: Which meaning fits «the clarinet's ligature held the reed firmly»? | A tie for a blood vessel | A loud note | *A clamp that holds the reed | A joined pair of letters
